// Short texts about every dish: where it really comes from and what makes it special, two or three sentences each.
// Shown on Cookbook cards (also before the dish is discovered, as a clue), in the Codex, in the Test Kitchen and on
// restaurant menus. Copied to FS.items[id].about in unlocks.js; tools/validate.js checks that every dish has one.
// Where an origin is disputed, the text says so and names the main claims rather than picking one.
(function (FS) {
  FS.ABOUT = {
    // Farmhouse Kitchen
    'Boiled Eggs': "Eaten all over the world for as long as people have kept hens and had a pot of water. Soft or hard, it all comes down to the minutes: about four for a runny yolk, ten for a firm one.",
    'Scrambled Eggs': "A farmhouse breakfast found in almost every country, from British “scrambled” to French “oeufs brouillés”. Stirred gently over low heat in butter, the eggs set into soft, creamy curds.",
    'Omelette': "The word is French, but beaten eggs cooked flat in a pan are eaten everywhere from Spain to Japan. A French omelette is rolled while still soft inside and finished with fresh chives.",
    'Garden Salad': "Raw leaves and vegetables straight from the kitchen garden, eaten across Europe and the Americas. The word salad comes from the Latin “sal”, salt, which the Romans sprinkled on their greens.",
    'Baked Potato': "Potatoes came from the Andes of South America, where they were first farmed some 8,000 years ago, and reached Europe in the 1500s. Baked whole in their skins, they became a cheap and filling meal in Britain and Ireland.",
    'Mashed Potatoes': "A comfort food of Britain, Ireland, Germany and North America, made once the South American potato had become a European staple. Boiled potatoes are mashed with butter and milk until fluffy.",
    'Buttered Toast': "Toasting stale bread over a fire is ancient; the toast-and-butter breakfast became a British and American habit. Butter itself is made by churning cream until the fat clumps together.",
    'Ham & Cheese Sandwich': "Named after the 4th Earl of Sandwich, an English nobleman said to have eaten meat between bread so he never had to leave the card table. Ham and cheese is one of the most common fillings in Europe and the Americas.",
    'Grilled Cheese Sandwich': "Cheese melted on bread is old, but the buttered, pan-fried grilled cheese is an American classic of the 1920s, when sliced bread and processed cheese became cheap. Today it is comfort food across the English-speaking world.",
    'Vegetable Soup': "Every farming culture simmers its vegetables in water or stock, so this soup has no single birthplace. Root vegetables and leeks keep through winter, which made it a cold-season staple in European farmhouses.",
    'Roasted Vegetables': "Root vegetables roasted in fat until their sugars caramelise, a staple of British and northern European home cooking. Parsnips and beetroot taste sweeter after a frost, when they turn some starch into sugar.",
    'Fruit Salad': "Mixed fresh fruit sweetened with honey is made all over the world, wherever orchards and gardens give fruit at the same time. Honey was the main sweetener in Europe before sugar became cheap in the 1800s.",
    'Porridge': "Oats cooked in milk or water, eaten for centuries in Scotland, Ireland and Scandinavia, where oats grow well in cool, wet weather. Scots traditionally stir it with a wooden stick called a spurtle.",
    // Swiss Alpine
    'Rösti': "A Swiss potato cake from the canton of Bern, first eaten by farmers for breakfast. It is so typical that the line between French- and German-speaking Switzerland is jokingly called the “Röstigraben”, the rösti ditch.",
    'Cheese Fondue': "A Swiss dish from the western Alps, where Gruyère and Emmental are made, also eaten in neighbouring French Savoy. Bread is dipped into cheese melted in white wine; whoever loses their bread in the pot pays a forfeit.",
    'Raclette': "From the canton of Valais in Switzerland: a half wheel of cheese is held to the fire and the melted layer is scraped onto potatoes. The name comes from the French “racler”, to scrape.",
    'Älplermagronen': "“Alpine herdsmen's macaroni” from central Switzerland, cooked in mountain huts from what the herders had: pasta, potatoes, cheese and cream. It is always served with applesauce on the side.",
    'Käsespätzle': "Soft egg noodles layered with cheese and fried onions, from the Allgäu and Swabia in southern Germany and from Vorarlberg in western Austria. The dough is pressed or scraped straight into boiling water.",
    'Bircher Muesli': "A Swiss breakfast of soaked oats, grated apple and nuts, served at a Zurich health clinic. “Müesli” is Swiss German for “little mush”.",
    'Bündner Gerstensuppe': "A thick barley soup from the canton of Graubünden in eastern Switzerland. Barley grows high in the Alps where wheat struggles, and the soup is often cooked with air-dried beef or smoked pork.",
    'Capuns': "A dish from the Surselva region of Graubünden, Switzerland, whose name is Romansh, the fourth national language. Every family has its own recipe for the little chard parcels, simmered in milk and stock.",
    'Zürcher Geschnetzeltes': "Thin strips of veal in a creamy mushroom and white wine sauce, from the city of Zurich in Switzerland. It is traditionally served with rösti.",
    'Lovage Potato Soup': "A creamy potato soup flavoured with lovage, an old garden herb grown across Switzerland, Germany and Central Europe. Lovage tastes so much like stock that in German it is called “Maggikraut”, after the Swiss soup brand.",
    'Spelt Waffles': "Waffles made with spelt, an ancient wheat that has been grown in Switzerland and southern Germany since prehistoric times. Spelt was almost forgotten in the 1900s but has made a comeback in Alpine bakeries.",
    'Anisbrötli': "Swiss aniseed biscuits pressed into carved wooden moulds, a Christmas tradition from Basel and German-speaking Switzerland. They dry overnight before baking, so they rise with a pale top and a little “foot”.",
    // Nordic & Dutch
    'Swedish Meatballs': "Sweden's köttbullar, served with gravy, mashed potatoes and lingonberry jam. A popular story says King Charles XII brought the idea back from Turkey in the 1700s; whether that is true or not, they are now a Swedish emblem.",
    'Gravlax': "Raw salmon cured with salt, sugar and dill, from Scandinavia. Medieval fishermen buried salted salmon in the sand to ferment it; “grav” means grave or hole, and “lax” means salmon.",
    'Kanelbullar': "Swedish cinnamon buns with cardamom in the dough, first baked in the 1920s. They are so loved that Sweden celebrates Cinnamon Bun Day every 4 October.",
    'Smørrebrød': "Danish open sandwiches on buttered rye bread, from Copenhagen lunch tables of the 1800s. The name simply means “butter and bread”, and there are strict rules about which toppings go together.",
    'Æbleskiver': "Round Danish pancake puffs, eaten in Denmark in the weeks before Christmas with jam and powdered sugar. Despite the name, “apple slices”, there is usually no apple in them today.",
    "Jansson's Temptation": "A Swedish potato gratin with onions, cream and spiced sprats, eaten at Christmas and Easter. Nobody is sure which Jansson it is named after; one story says an opera singer, another a 1920s film.",
    'Pickled Herring on Rye': "Herring preserved in vinegar and spices is a staple of Scandinavia, the Netherlands, Germany and Poland. The Baltic and North Sea herring trade fed much of medieval Europe and made the Hanseatic towns rich.",
    'Karelian Pies': "Thin rye crusts filled with rice porridge from Karelia, a region now split between Finland and Russia. In Finland they are eaten with egg butter, a mix of chopped boiled egg and butter.",
    'Norwegian Fish Soup': "A creamy soup of cod and vegetables from the coast of Norway, where cod has been fished and dried for over a thousand years. The cod come each winter to spawn around the Lofoten islands.",
    'Stamppot': "A Dutch winter dish of potatoes mashed with vegetables, here curly kale, served with a smoked sausage. Its ancestor, hutspot, is linked to the Siege of Leiden in 1574.",
    'Gouda & Apple Sandwich': "A Dutch lunch of brown bread with Gouda, apple and mustard. Gouda cheese is named after the town in South Holland where farmers have traded it at the cheese market since the Middle Ages.",
    // British & Irish
    'Full English Breakfast': "England's cooked breakfast of bacon, sausage, eggs, beans, mushrooms and toast, which grew from the country house breakfasts of the 1800s. Ireland, Scotland and Wales each have their own version.",
    'Fish and Chips': "Britain's takeaway classic, first sold in London and the north of England in the 1860s. Fried fish came with Portuguese and Spanish Jewish refugees; chips came from the potato trade. London and Lancashire both claim the first shop.",
    'Roast Chicken': "Chicken roasted whole with butter, garlic and herbs: the Sunday lunch of Britain, France and many other countries. Until the 1950s chicken was an expensive treat, kept mostly for its eggs.",
    'Roast Beef & Yorkshire Pudding': "The Sunday roast of England, with Yorkshire pudding from the north of England, a batter baked in beef fat. It was first served before the meat, so a filling pudding stretched a small joint further.",
    'Roast Leg of Lamb': "A British and Irish Sunday and Easter roast, studded with garlic and rosemary. Anchovies melt away in the oven and leave a deep, savoury flavour without tasting of fish.",
    'Lamb Chops with Mint Sauce': "Mint sauce with lamb is a very British pairing, rarely found elsewhere. The sharp sauce of chopped mint, vinegar and sugar cuts through the fat of the meat.",
    'Cottage Pie': "A British pie of minced beef under a crust of mashed potato, from the 1700s, when potatoes became the cheap food of country cottages. With lamb instead of beef it becomes shepherd's pie.",
    "Shepherd's Pie": "Minced lamb under mashed potato, from the sheep-farming north of England and Scotland. It was a thrifty way of using up the leftovers of the Sunday roast.",
    'Cornish Pasty': "A pastry parcel of beef, potato, swede and onion from Cornwall in south-west England, the lunch of tin miners. The thick crimped edge is said to have been a handle for dirty hands.",
    'Bangers and Mash': "British sausages with mashed potatoes and onion gravy. During the World Wars sausages were stretched with so much water that they burst with a bang in the pan.",
    'Irish Stew': "Ireland's national stew of mutton or lamb, potatoes and onions, simmered slowly in one pot. Purists say carrots do not belong in it, but many families add them.",
    'Scotch Broth': "A thick Scottish soup of barley, lamb or mutton and root vegetables. It is a filling meal for a cold climate and gets better when warmed up the next day.",
    'Cullen Skink': "A creamy soup of smoked haddock, potato and onion from the fishing village of Cullen in north-east Scotland. “Skink” was an old Scots word for a soup made from beef shin.",
    'Kedgeree': "A British breakfast of rice, smoked fish, eggs and curry powder, adapted by colonial officials from khichdi, an Indian rice and lentil dish. It became fashionable at Victorian country house breakfasts.",
    'Welsh Rarebit': "A Welsh dish of melted cheese with beer and mustard poured over toast. First written down in the 1700s as “Welsh rabbit”, though it has never contained any rabbit.",
    'Potted Shrimp': "Tiny brown shrimps from Morecambe Bay in Lancashire, England, sealed in spiced butter. Potting under butter was a way of keeping food before refrigerators.",
    'Sardines on Toast': "A quick British and Irish meal straight from the tin. Canning was invented in the early 1800s to feed armies and sailors, and Portugal and Brittany became famous for canned sardines.",
    'Watercress Soup': "A green English soup of watercress, grown in clear chalk streams in Hampshire. Watercress was such a common street food in Victorian London that it was called “poor man's bread”.",
    'Brussels Sprouts with Bacon': "A Christmas side dish of Britain and North America. The sprouts are named after Brussels in Belgium, where they were grown in the Middle Ages.",
    'Marmalade Toast': "A British breakfast. Bitter Seville oranges from Spain were boiled into a sweet preserve; tradition credits a Dundee family in Scotland with making it a breakfast staple.",
    'Scones with Jam & Cream': "The cream tea of Devon and Cornwall in south-west England, with clotted cream and strawberry jam. The two counties argue over whether jam or cream goes on first.",
    'Victoria Sponge': "A British cake of two sponges filled with jam and cream, named after Queen Victoria, who is said to have enjoyed a slice with her afternoon tea. Her lady-in-waiting, the Duchess of Bedford, is credited with starting the English habit of afternoon tea.",
    'Trifle': "An English layered dessert of sponge, fruit, jelly, custard and cream, known since the 1500s. Its name comes from an old word for something of little importance.",
    'Apple Pie': "Apple pies were baked in England as early as 1381. Settlers took the recipe to North America, where “as American as apple pie” became a saying, though neither apples nor pie started there.",
    'Sticky Toffee Pudding': "A date sponge drowned in toffee sauce from the north of England. It was made famous in the 1970s at the Sharrow Bay hotel in the Lake District, though older recipes are claimed too.",
    'Rhubarb Crumble': "A British dessert said to have spread during Second World War rationing, because a crumbly topping saved flour, fat and sugar compared to pastry. Forced pink rhubarb is grown in the dark in Yorkshire's “Rhubarb Triangle”.",
    'Apple & Blackberry Crumble': "A British autumn dessert: blackberries are picked from hedgerows just as the first apples ripen. The crumble topping became popular during wartime rationing in the 1940s.",
    'Cup of Tea': "Tea came from China and was made fashionable in Britain by Queen Catherine of Braganza in the 1660s. Britain and Ireland drink it strong with milk, and the Irish drink among the most tea per person in the world.",
    'Elderflower Cordial': "A sweet syrup of elderflower blossom made in British and Central European homes in early summer. The flowers are picked from wild hedgerow elder bushes and steeped with lemon and sugar.",
    // French & Belgian
    'Steak Frites': "A bistro favourite claimed by both France and Belgium. Belgians say they invented frites, fried twice for a crisp outside; the French name “French fries” may only mean that they are cut into strips.",
    'French Onion Soup': "A Paris classic of onions slowly caramelised in beef stock under a crust of bread and melted cheese. It was the late-night meal of workers and night owls at the old Les Halles market.",
    'Coq au Vin': "A French country dish of chicken braised in red wine with bacon and mushrooms, linked to Burgundy and Auvergne. Long cooking in wine was the way to make a tough old rooster tender.",
    'Cassoulet': "A slow-baked casserole of white beans with duck, sausage and pork from the Languedoc in south-west France. Castelnaudary, Carcassonne and Toulouse each claim the true recipe.",
    'Ratatouille': "A summer vegetable stew from Nice and Provence in southern France. The name comes from an old French word for stirring, and each vegetable is often cooked separately first.",
    'Quiche Lorraine': "A savoury tart of eggs, cream and bacon from Lorraine in north-east France, where it began as an open bread dough pie. The name quiche may come from the German “Kuchen”, cake.",
    'Tarte Flambée': "A thin bread with cream, onions and bacon from Alsace in eastern France, also baked across the Rhine in Baden, Germany. Its local name is Flammekueche.",
    'Croque Monsieur': "A toasted ham and cheese sandwich with béchamel, first served in Paris cafés around 1910. With a fried egg on top it becomes a croque madame.",
    'Galette Complète': "A thin buckwheat pancake from Brittany in north-west France, filled with egg, ham and cheese. Buckwheat grows well in Brittany's poor, acidic soils, where wheat does not.",
    'Salade Niçoise': "A salad of tuna, eggs, olives, anchovies and vegetables from the city of Nice on the French Riviera. Locals argue fiercely about what belongs in it; boiled potatoes and green beans are disputed.",
    'Moules Marinières': "Mussels steamed in white wine, shallots and garlic, eaten along the coasts of France and in Belgium, where they come with fries as moules-frites. Mussels are farmed on ropes or wooden posts in the sea.",
    'Gratin Dauphinois': "Thinly sliced potatoes baked slowly in cream and garlic, from the Dauphiné region in the French Alps around Grenoble. Purists say a true dauphinois has no cheese.",
    'Celeriac Rémoulade': "A classic French starter of raw celeriac in a mustard mayonnaise, sold in every Paris delicatessen. Celeriac is a celery grown for its knobbly root.",
    'Asparagus with Hollandaise': "Spring white asparagus with ham, potatoes and hollandaise sauce, a seasonal feast in Alsace, Belgium, the Netherlands and Germany. Despite its name, hollandaise sauce is French.",
    'Chanterelles on Toast': "Golden wild mushrooms fried in butter on toast, a late-summer treat across France, Central Europe and Scandinavia. Chanterelles cannot be farmed; they grow only with the roots of living trees.",
    'Tarte Tatin': "An upside-down caramelised apple tart from the Hotel Tatin in Lamotte-Beuvron, central France, around 1880. Legend says one of the Tatin sisters put the pastry on top by mistake.",
    'Madeleines': "Small shell-shaped sponge cakes from Commercy in Lorraine, north-east France. The writer Marcel Proust made them famous when the taste of one dipped in tea brought back his childhood.",
    'Far Breton': "A dense custard flan with prunes from Brittany in north-west France. “Far” comes from the Latin word for spelt flour; it began as a savoury porridge before sugar and prunes were added.",
    'Crêpes': "Thin pancakes from Brittany in north-west France, now sold on street corners all over the country. France eats them on Candlemas, 2 February, when tradition says you flip one holding a coin for luck.",
    'Hazelnut Crêpes': "French crêpes filled with hazelnut cocoa spread and banana, a modern street food favourite. Hazelnut-chocolate spreads come from Piedmont in Italy, where hazelnuts were mixed in to stretch scarce cocoa.",
    'Waffles': "Belgium's waffle tradition goes back to medieval wafer irons; Brussels waffles are light and crisp, Liège waffles dense and sugary. Belgian immigrants made them famous at the 1964 New York World's Fair.",
    // German, Austrian & Hungarian
    'Wiener Schnitzel': "Vienna's veal escalope, beaten thin, breaded and fried in butter or lard. By Austrian law, only a veal schnitzel may be called Wiener Schnitzel; similar breaded cutlets also exist in Milan.",
    'Bratwurst with Sauerkraut': "German grilled sausages with fermented cabbage, mustard and potatoes. Nuremberg, Thuringia and Franconia each have their own bratwurst; Thuringia's is first recorded in 1404.",
    'Soft Pretzels': "Twisted breads from southern Germany, Bavaria and Swabia, also loved in Austria and Alsace. Dipping them in lye before baking gives the shiny brown crust; monasteries were baking pretzels in the Middle Ages.",
    'Tafelspitz': "Vienna's boiled beef, served with its broth, apple and horseradish sauce and potatoes. Emperor Franz Joseph is said to have eaten it nearly every day.",
    'Roast Goose with Red Cabbage': "A feast dish of Germany, Austria and Central Europe, eaten on St Martin's Day in November and at Christmas. Geese were fattened on the stubble fields after the grain harvest.",
    'Rabbit Stew': "Rabbit braised in wine and mustard, cooked in farmhouses across Germany, France and Belgium. Rabbits were kept in hutches in many backyards as a cheap source of meat.",
    'Hungarian Goulash': "A beef soup from Hungary, first cooked in kettles by cattle herders, the gulyás, on the Great Hungarian Plain. Paprika, from peppers brought from the Americas, gives its red colour.",
    'Chicken Paprikash': "A Hungarian chicken stew in a sauce of paprika and sour cream, served with small dumplings. Hungary's paprika is grown around Szeged and Kalocsa.",
    'Lángos': "Hungarian deep-fried flatbread, rubbed with garlic and topped with sour cream and cheese. It was once baked at the edge of the bread oven and is now a favourite at beaches and markets.",
    'Czech Christmas Carp': "In the Czech Republic and Slovakia, breaded fried carp with potato salad is the Christmas Eve dinner. Families traditionally bought a live carp and kept it in the bathtub until the big day.",
    'Styrian Pumpkin Soup': "A creamy pumpkin soup from Styria in south-east Austria, finished with dark green pumpkin seed oil. The oil comes from a pumpkin with hull-less seeds that was bred there in the 1800s.",
    'Apple Strudel': "Vienna's apple pastry, stretched so thin you could read a newspaper through it. The idea of thin pastry came through Hungary from the Ottoman kitchen; the oldest strudel recipe, from 1696, is in Vienna's library.",
    'Linzer Torte': "A lattice-topped nut and jam tart from Linz in Upper Austria. A recipe from 1653 is why it is often called the oldest named cake in the world.",
    'Kaiserschmarrn': "A thick torn pancake with raisins and plum jam from Austria. The name means “emperor's mess”; it is linked to Emperor Franz Joseph, though stories differ about how.",
    'Mohnkuchen': "A poppy seed cake loved in Germany, Austria, Poland and the Czech Republic. Bakers grind the tiny blue seeds to release their nutty oil.",
    'Thumbprint Cookies': "Butter biscuits with a dab of jam pressed into a thumbprint, baked for Christmas in Austria and Germany, where they are called Husarenkrapferl, “hussar's doughnuts”. Swedes bake a similar cookie called hallongrottor, “raspberry caves”.",
    'Mulled Wine': "Hot spiced red wine, sold at Christmas markets in Germany and Austria as Glühwein, “glow wine”. Spiced wine goes back to the Romans; Nordic countries have their own glögg.",
    // Italian
    'Pizza Margherita': "The classic pizza of Naples in southern Italy, with tomato, mozzarella and basil. The craft of the Neapolitan pizza maker is on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage.",
    'Pizza Marinara': "The oldest of the Neapolitan pizzas, from the 1700s: just tomato, garlic, oregano and olive oil. It is named after the wives of fishermen, the marinara, who made it for their husbands.",
    'Pizza Quattro Formaggi': "A white Italian pizza with four cheeses, from mild mozzarella to sharp blue. It is popular all over Italy, though it is newer than the Neapolitan classics.",
    'Pizza Prosciutto e Funghi': "An Italian pizzeria favourite with cooked ham and mushrooms. Ham and mushrooms appear on pizzas from Naples to Rome.",
    'Pepperoni Pizza': "America's favourite pizza, from New York, where Italian immigrants opened pizzerias from 1905. Pepperoni is an Italian-American spicy salami; in Italy “peperoni” means bell peppers.",
    'Spaghetti Carbonara': "A Roman pasta of eggs, guanciale (cured pork cheek), pecorino and black pepper, first recorded in the 1950s. One theory links it to American soldiers' bacon and egg rations in 1944 Rome.",
    'Tagliatelle al Ragù': "Bologna's own pasta, with its slowly simmered meat ragù. Italy's Academy of Cuisine registered an official ragù recipe in Bologna in 1982; spaghetti bolognese is an invention from abroad.",
    'Lasagna alla Bolognese': "Layers of pasta, ragù and béchamel from Bologna in Emilia-Romagna, northern Italy. Green spinach pasta sheets are traditional there.",
    'Spaghetti Aglio e Olio': "A Neapolitan pasta of garlic, olive oil and chili, from the cheapest ingredients in the pantry. It is the classic midnight snack of Italian cooks after work.",
    'Cacio e Pepe': "A Roman pasta of just pecorino cheese and black pepper, tossed with starchy pasta water into a cream. Shepherds of the Roman countryside could carry these ingredients for weeks.",
    "Penne all'Arrabbiata": "“Angry” pasta from Rome, named for the heat of its chili-spiked tomato sauce. It is a classic of the Lazio region around Rome.",
    'Penne alla Vodka': "A creamy tomato pasta with a splash of vodka, fashionable in Italy and the USA in the 1980s. Several restaurants in Italy and New York claim to have invented it.",
    'Fettuccine Alfredo': "Created around 1914 by Alfredo di Lelio in Rome, who served pasta with lots of butter and parmesan to his wife after childbirth. It became famous in America after Hollywood stars ate at his restaurant.",
    'Trofie al Pesto': "Twisted pasta with pesto, potatoes and green beans, from Liguria on the Italian Riviera around Genoa. Pesto is pounded in a marble mortar from basil, pine nuts, cheese and garlic.",
    'Spaghetti alle Vongole': "Spaghetti with clams, white wine and garlic from Naples. On Christmas Eve, when many Italians eat no meat, it is a classic.",
    'Wild Garlic Spaghetti': "Pasta tossed with wild garlic, gathered in spring woodland across Italy, Germany, Switzerland and the UK. Wild garlic is also known as ramsons or bear's garlic.",
    'Spinach & Ricotta Ravioli': "Pasta parcels filled with spinach and ricotta, eaten across northern Italy, especially Emilia-Romagna and Liguria. Butter and sage is the classic simple sauce.",
    'Gnocchi al Pomodoro': "Small potato dumplings with tomato sauce from Italy, where potato gnocchi became common in the 1800s. Rome traditionally eats gnocchi on Thursdays.",
    'Polenta e Funghi': "Cornmeal porridge with mushrooms from the Alpine north of Italy: Veneto, Lombardy and Friuli. Maize came from the Americas and became the staple food of poor northern Italian farmers.",
    'Arancini': "Fried rice balls from Sicily, often stuffed with ragù, peas and cheese. Their name means “little oranges”; rice was brought to Sicily by Arab rulers in the 10th century.",
    'Saltimbocca alla Romana': "Rome's veal escalopes topped with prosciutto and sage, fried in butter and white wine. The name means “jumps in the mouth”.",
    'Eggplant Parmigiana': "Layers of fried eggplant, tomato and cheese baked in the oven, claimed by both Sicily and Naples. The name may come from Parma, or from a Sicilian word for shutter slats.",
    'Minestrone': "A thick Italian vegetable soup with beans and pasta, made in every region with what is in season. It has been a peasant meal since Roman times.",
    'Caprese Salad': "Tomato, mozzarella and basil from the island of Capri, near Naples. Its colours match the Italian flag.",
    'Bruschetta': "Grilled bread rubbed with garlic and topped with tomato and olive oil, from central Italy. It was a way for olive farmers to taste the new season's oil.",
    'Focaccia': "A flat, olive-oiled bread from Liguria in north-west Italy, Genoa above all. Bakers press dimples into the dough to hold pools of olive oil.",
    'Antipasto Platter': "Italian cured meats, cheese, olives and preserved vegetables served before a meal. “Antipasto” means “before the meal”.",
    'Prosciutto e Melone': "Sweet melon wrapped in salty prosciutto, an Italian summer starter. Prosciutto di Parma is air-dried for a year or more in the hills near Parma.",
    'Persimmon & Burrata Salad': "A modern Italian autumn salad of persimmon and creamy cheese. Persimmons came from China and Japan but are grown in southern Italy, where they are called cachi.",
    'Stuffed Artichokes': "Artichokes stuffed with breadcrumbs, garlic and cheese, from Sicily and southern Italy. Artichokes are the flower buds of a thistle, grown around the Mediterranean since antiquity.",
    'Panna Cotta': "“Cooked cream” from Piedmont in northern Italy, set with gelatin and served with fruit or caramel. It became popular across Italy only in the 1990s.",
    'Affogato': "Italian vanilla gelato “drowned” in a shot of hot espresso. Italy's gelato tradition goes back to Florence and Sicily in the 1500s.",
    'Espresso': "Strong coffee made by pushing hot water at high pressure through finely ground beans, invented in Turin and Milan around 1900. Coffee itself first grew in Ethiopia.",
    'Cappuccino': "An Italian espresso with steamed and foamed milk, named after the brown robes of Capuchin friars. Italians traditionally drink it only in the morning.",
    // Mediterranean & Middle Eastern
    'Paella Valenciana': "Spain's famous rice dish from the rice fields around Valencia, cooked over an open fire in a wide, flat pan. The original has chicken, rabbit and green beans, not seafood, and the crispy bottom layer is called socarrat.",
    'Seafood Paella': "The seaside version of Valencia's paella, made all along Spain's Mediterranean coast with prawns, mussels and squid. Saffron, the world's most expensive spice, gives the rice its golden colour.",
    'Gazpacho': "A cold soup of raw tomato, cucumber, peppers and bread from Andalusia in southern Spain, made for the scorching summers. Before tomatoes arrived from the Americas it was a soup of bread, oil, garlic and vinegar.",
    'Tortilla Española': "Spain's potato and onion omelette, cooked thick and flipped with a plate. Whether it should have onion splits Spain; one story places its birth in Navarre during the Carlist wars of the 1830s.",
    'Patatas Bravas': "Fried potatoes with a spicy tomato and paprika sauce, a tapa from Madrid. “Bravas” means fierce, for the heat of the sauce.",
    'Gambas al Ajillo': "Prawns sizzled in olive oil with lots of garlic and chili, a tapa from Madrid and southern Spain. It is served still bubbling in a small clay dish, with bread to mop up the oil.",
    'Pulpo a la Gallega': "Octopus from Galicia in north-west Spain, boiled in a copper pot, sliced and dressed with olive oil, salt and paprika. It is the star of Galician village fairs, served on wooden plates.",
    'Calamares Fritos': "Squid rings fried in light batter, eaten all along Spain's coasts. In Madrid, far from the sea, they are even stuffed into a bread roll as a bocadillo.",
    'Fabada Asturiana': "A rich bean stew from Asturias in northern Spain, cooked with big white fabes beans, chorizo, black pudding and pork. It is a hearty meal for the rainy, green north.",
    'Bacalhau à Brás': "Shredded salt cod with fried potato straws and egg, from Lisbon in Portugal, said to be named after a tavern owner called Brás. The Portuguese say they have a cod recipe for every day of the year.",
    'Pan con Tomate': "Toasted bread rubbed with ripe tomato, garlic and olive oil, from Catalonia in north-east Spain, where it is called pa amb tomàquet. It was a way to soften stale bread.",
    'Jamón & Manchego Board': "A Spanish tapas board of cured ham, Manchego sheep's cheese from La Mancha and sweet quince paste. Spanish hams hang to dry for up to three years.",
    'Churros con Chocolate': "Fried dough sticks dipped in thick hot chocolate, a Spanish breakfast and late-night snack, famous in Madrid. Some say shepherds invented them; others say Portuguese sailors brought the idea from China.",
    'Crema Catalana': "Catalonia's custard with a burnt sugar top, flavoured with lemon and cinnamon, traditionally eaten on St Joseph's Day, 19 March. Catalans say it is older than French crème brûlée.",
    'Sangria': "Red wine punch with chopped fruit and brandy from Spain and Portugal. Its name comes from “sangre”, blood, for its colour.",
    'Greek Salad': "Horiatiki, the “village salad” of Greece: tomato, cucumber, onion, olives and a slab of feta. It became popular in Greek tavernas in the 1960s and 70s.",
    'Moussaka': "A Greek bake of eggplant, spiced lamb and béchamel. Its modern form was created by the Greek chef Nikolaos Tselementes in the 1920s; older moussakas without béchamel are made from the Balkans to the Middle East.",
    'Spanakopita': "A Greek pie of spinach and feta in crisp filo pastry. Similar pies are baked all over the former Ottoman lands, from the Balkans to Turkey.",
    'Souvlaki with Tzatziki': "Greek grilled meat skewers wrapped in pita with tzatziki, a yogurt, cucumber and garlic sauce. Ancient Greeks already grilled meat on skewers called obeliskoi.",
    'Halloumi Salad': "Halloumi, the squeaky grilling cheese from Cyprus, served with watermelon and mint. Halloumi keeps its shape over the flame because it is heated during cheesemaking.",
    'Hummus': "A creamy dip of chickpeas, tahini and lemon from the Levant, eaten in Lebanon, Syria, Palestine, Israel and Jordan. Several countries claim it; the first written recipes come from 13th century Cairo.",
    'Falafel': "Fried balls of ground chickpeas or fava beans and herbs. Most food historians think falafel began in Egypt, where it is made with fava beans; the chickpea version is the street food of the Levant.",
    'Tabbouleh': "A Lebanese and Syrian salad that is mostly parsley, with a little bulgur, mint, tomato and lemon. It began in the mountains of Lebanon and Syria.",
    'Fattoush': "A Levantine salad from Lebanon and Syria with crisp pieces of toasted or fried pita and sour sumac. It was a way to use up yesterday's bread.",
    'Baba Ganoush': "A Levantine dip of smoky charred eggplant with tahini and lemon, eaten across Lebanon, Syria and their neighbours. One story says the name means “pampered father”.",
    'Shakshuka': "Eggs poached in a spiced tomato and pepper sauce, from North Africa: Tunisia, Libya, Algeria and Morocco. Jewish immigrants from North Africa made it a favourite breakfast in Israel.",
    'Chicken Shawarma': "Marinated meat stacked on a vertical spit and shaved off as it roasts, from the Ottoman doner kebab of the 1800s. As shawarma it became the street food of the Levant.",
    'Kofta Kebab': "Spiced minced meat shaped on skewers and grilled, made from the Balkans through Turkey and the Middle East to India. The name comes from the Persian “kufta”, ground meat.",
    'Lahmacun': "A thin, crisp flatbread topped with spiced minced meat, from south-east Turkey, Armenia and Syria. It is rolled up with parsley and lemon and eaten by hand.",
    "Manakish Za'atar": "A Lebanese flatbread spread with za'atar, a mix of wild thyme, sumac and sesame, and olive oil. It is the classic breakfast of Lebanon and the Levant, baked in neighbourhood ovens.",
    'Mujaddara': "Lentils and rice topped with crispy fried onions, eaten across the Levant and Egypt. A recipe appears in a Baghdad cookbook from 1226, and it is cheap enough to have been called the poor man's meal.",
    'Ful Medames': "Slow-cooked fava beans with olive oil, lemon and cumin, Egypt's national breakfast. The beans are traditionally simmered overnight in a big pot.",
    'Sabich': "An Israeli pita sandwich of fried eggplant, hard-boiled egg and tahini. It became a Tel Aviv street food in the 1940s and 50s.",
    'Menemen': "Turkish scrambled eggs cooked in tomatoes, green peppers and chili, served in the pan for breakfast. Turks argue whether onions belong in it.",
    'Vegetable Couscous': "Steamed semolina grains with a vegetable and chickpea stew, the staple of the Maghreb. Algeria, Morocco, Tunisia and Mauritania jointly put couscous on UNESCO's heritage list in 2020.",
    'Turkish Delight': "Lokum, soft cubes of starch and sugar from Ottoman Istanbul. The confectioner Bekir Efendi made it famous in the late 1700s; his shop still stands.",
    'Moroccan Mint Tea': "Green tea brewed with fresh mint and lots of sugar, the welcome drink of Morocco. Green tea came with British traders in the 1700s and 1800s; it is poured from high up to make a foam.",
    'Citrus Salad': "Sliced oranges, grapefruit and mandarins with mint and honey, a simple dessert of Morocco and the Mediterranean. Sweet oranges came from China via Portuguese traders in the 1500s.",
    // Around the World
    'Pierogi': "Half-moon dumplings from Poland, here filled with potato and cheese, called “ruskie” pierogi. Similar dumplings are made in Ukraine as varenyky.",
    'Borscht': "A sour beetroot soup from Ukraine, eaten across Eastern Europe in Russia, Poland, Belarus and Lithuania. In 2022 UNESCO listed Ukrainian borscht cooking as heritage in need of urgent safeguarding.",
    'Beef Stroganoff': "Strips of beef in a sour cream and mustard sauce from Russia, named after the noble Stroganov family. It was first published in a Russian cookbook in 1871 and spread worldwide with Russian emigrants.",
    'Chicken Kiev': "A breaded chicken breast with a heart of garlic butter that spurts when cut. Its origin is disputed: it may come from French cooking in 19th century Russia, and Kyiv in Ukraine also claims it.",
    'Pelmeni': "Small meat dumplings from Siberia and the Urals in Russia, served with sour cream. The name may come from the Komi and Udmurt words for “bread ear”.",
    'Gołąbki': "Cabbage leaves rolled around minced meat and rice and baked in tomato sauce. Their name means “little pigeons”.",
    'Bigos': "Poland's national stew of sauerkraut, fresh cabbage, mixed meats and dried mushrooms. It is a winter and Christmas dish in Poland and Lithuania.",
    'Syrniki': "Fried pancakes of fresh curd cheese, eaten for breakfast in Ukraine, Russia and Belarus. The name comes from “syr”, the Slavic word for cheese.",
    'Olivier Salad': "A potato salad with pickles, eggs, peas and mayonnaise, created in Moscow by the chef Lucien Olivier at his Hermitage restaurant. Abroad it is often simply called Russian salad.",
    'Okroshka': "A cold summer soup from Russia of chopped vegetables, egg and ham in kefir or kvass, a fermented bread drink. The name comes from “kroshit”, to crumble.",
    'Ćevapi': "Small grilled sausages of minced meat from the Balkans, served in flatbread with onion and ajvar pepper relish. They are linked above all with Bosnia and Herzegovina and Serbia.",
    'Khachapuri': "Bread filled with melted cheese from Georgia in the Caucasus. Every region has its own shape, and economists in Georgia even track a “khachapuri index” of prices.",
    'Plov': "Rice cooked with lamb, carrots and onions from Central Asia, above all Uzbekistan, where plov culture is on UNESCO's heritage list. Related to Persian polow and Indian pulao.",
    'Jollof Rice': "Rice cooked in a spicy tomato and pepper sauce, from West Africa. It is named after the old Wolof (Jolof) Empire of Senegal, and Ghana and Nigeria argue hotly over who makes it best.",
    'Suya': "Spicy grilled beef skewers from northern Nigeria, made by Hausa grill masters. Today it is sold at roadside stands and night markets across Nigeria, Ghana and Cameroon.",
    'Yam Pottage': "Yams simmered in a spicy palm oil and tomato sauce, called asaro in Nigeria. West Africa grows most of the world's yams; the New Yam Festival celebrates the harvest.",
    'Fufu with Light Soup': "Pounded cassava and plantain served with a light, spicy goat soup, from Ghana. The dough is pounded in a big mortar and eaten by hand, dipping each piece in the soup.",
    'Kelewele': "Spicy fried plantain cubes with ginger and chili, a Ghanaian street snack sold in the evening. Plantains are cooking bananas, starchier and less sweet than the dessert kind.",
    'Maafe': "A peanut stew from Mali and Senegal, made by the Mandinka and Bambara peoples. Peanuts came from South America, but West Africa made them its own.",
    'Misir Wot': "A red lentil stew from Ethiopia and Eritrea, spiced with berbere and eaten on injera, a spongy sourdough flatbread made of teff. It is a fasting dish for the many meat-free days of the Ethiopian Orthodox church.",
    'Bobotie': "South Africa's spiced minced meat bake with a golden egg custard topping. It came with Cape Malay cooks, many of them brought as enslaved people from Indonesia by the Dutch.",
    'Chakalaka': "A spicy South African vegetable relish of beans, carrots and peppers, said to have been invented in the townships around Johannesburg by gold miners. It is served at every braai, or barbecue.",
    'Shakshouka Tunisienne': "Shakshouka is widely thought to have started in Tunisia. This version is spiced with caraway and harissa, Tunisia's fiery chili paste, which UNESCO listed as Tunisian heritage in 2022.",
    'Aussie Meat Pie': "A hand-sized pie of minced meat and gravy, Australia's favourite snack at football matches, and New Zealand's too. It came with British settlers and is eaten with tomato sauce on top.",
    'Grilled Barramundi': "Barramundi is a big fish from northern Australia; its name comes from an Aboriginal language of Queensland and means “large-scaled river fish”. It lives in both fresh and salt water.",
    'Anzac Biscuits': "Oat and golden syrup biscuits from Australia and New Zealand, named after the ANZAC soldiers of the First World War. They are baked every year for Anzac Day, 25 April.",
    'Pavlova': "A meringue cake with a crisp shell, soft middle, cream and fruit, named after the Russian ballerina Anna Pavlova after her 1920s tours. Australia and New Zealand both claim to have invented it, and the argument is still going.",
    'Hokey Pokey Ice Cream': "Vanilla ice cream with crunchy honeycomb toffee from New Zealand, first sold in the mid-1900s. “Hokey pokey” was a Victorian street name for cheap ice cream.",
    // East Asian
    'Egg Fried Rice': "Fried rice with egg, from China, where it was made to use up yesterday's cold rice. Yangzhou in Jiangsu is famous for its version.",
    'Kung Pao Chicken': "A spicy chicken stir-fry with peanuts and chilies from Sichuan in south-west China. It is named after a Qing dynasty governor, Ding Baozhen, whose title was Gongbao.",
    'Mapo Tofu': "Tofu in a fiery sauce of chili bean paste and numbing Sichuan pepper, from Chengdu in Sichuan, China. The name means “pockmarked old woman's tofu”, after the cook said to have created it in the 1800s.",
    'Sweet and Sour Pork': "Crispy pork in a sweet and tangy sauce from Guangdong (Canton), southern China. Cantonese emigrants made it one of the best-known Chinese dishes abroad.",
    'Char Siu': "Cantonese barbecued pork glazed with honey and hoisin, from Guangdong and Hong Kong. Its name means “fork roasted”, from roasting it on long forks.",
    'Twice-Cooked Pork': "Pork belly boiled and then stir-fried with chili bean paste and leeks, from Sichuan, China. It was once made from the meat offered to ancestors and then eaten by the family.",
    'Beef with Broccoli': "An American-Chinese stir-fry made by Cantonese cooks in the USA, who swapped Chinese gai lan for western broccoli. It is rarely found in China itself.",
    'Chicken Chow Mein': "Stir-fried noodles from southern China; the name means “fried noodles” in Taishanese. Chinese immigrants took it to America and Britain in the 1800s.",
    'Dan Dan Noodles': "Spicy Sichuan noodles with minced pork and chili oil from Chengdu, China. They are named after the carrying pole, the dan, that street sellers used to carry their kitchens.",
    'Jiaozi': "Northern Chinese dumplings eaten at Chinese New Year, when their shape recalls old gold ingots and brings wealth. Families fold them together on New Year's Eve.",
    'Wonton Soup': "Delicate dumplings of pork and shrimp in clear broth, a classic of Guangdong and Hong Kong in southern China. Wonton is the Cantonese way of saying “swallowing clouds”.",
    'Hot and Sour Soup': "A Chinese soup soured with vinegar and made hot with white pepper, linked to Sichuan and Beijing. Wood ear mushrooms give it its crunch.",
    'Spring Rolls': "Crispy fried rolls from China, eaten at the Spring Festival, the Chinese New Year, which gave them their name. Their golden colour stands for gold bars.",
    'Congee': "A soothing rice porridge eaten across China and East Asia, especially for breakfast or when ill. Cantonese congee is cooked until the rice grains dissolve completely.",
    'Egg Tarts': "Custard tarts from Hong Kong, created in the 1940s and inspired by British custard tarts. Macau's version, with flaky crust, follows the Portuguese pastel de nata.",
    'Garlic Snow Peas': "Snow peas stir-fried with garlic, a quick vegetable dish from southern China. The flat pods are eaten whole before the peas inside grow big.",
    'Lychee Sorbet': "An ice made from lychees, a fruit from southern China. A Tang dynasty emperor had fresh lychees rushed by horse relay over 1,000 km for his favourite consort.",
    'Pot of Green Tea': "Green tea comes from China, where people have drunk tea for more than 2,000 years. The leaves are heated soon after picking so they stay green.",
    'Miso Soup': "Japan's everyday soup of dashi broth and miso, a fermented soybean paste, with tofu and seaweed. Many Japanese still drink it with breakfast.",
    'Onigiri': "Japanese rice balls wrapped in nori seaweed, often with salmon inside. They have been a packed lunch since samurai times and are sold in every Japanese convenience store.",
    'Tuna Maki': "Thin sushi rolls with tuna from Japan, where rolled sushi appeared in Edo, today's Tokyo, in the 1700s. Wasabi is a grated root, and real wasabi is rare and expensive.",
    'California Roll': "An inside-out sushi roll with surimi crab, avocado and cucumber, with rice on the outside. Japanese chefs in North America created it; Hidekazu Tojo of Vancouver and Los Angeles restaurants both claim it.",
    'Teriyaki Salmon': "Fish grilled with a glossy soy, mirin and sugar glaze, a Japanese cooking style. “Teri” means shine and “yaki” means grill.",
    'Tonkatsu': "Japanese breaded pork cutlet, created in Tokyo in 1899 inspired by European cutlets. It is served with shredded cabbage and a thick fruity sauce.",
    'Chicken Katsu Curry': "Breaded chicken with Japanese curry. Curry came to Japan via the British Navy in the late 1800s and became one of Japan's favourite home dishes.",
    'Gyoza': "Pan-fried dumplings, crisp on the bottom, from Japan. Soldiers returning from Manchuria in China brought the recipe for jiaozi home after the Second World War.",
    'Yakitori': "Grilled chicken skewers from Japan, cooked over charcoal and brushed with a sweet soy glaze. They are the classic snack of izakaya pubs after work.",
    'Oyakodon': "A rice bowl from Tokyo with chicken and egg simmered in sweet dashi. The name means “parent and child bowl”, because it holds both chicken and egg.",
    'Gyudon': "A Japanese bowl of rice topped with thin beef and onion simmered in sweet soy. It became Japan's fast food after the Meiji era lifted the ban on eating beef.",
    'Shoyu Ramen': "Ramen in a clear chicken and soy sauce broth, the original Tokyo style. Ramen came to Japan with Chinese cooks around 1900; Japan made it its own.",
    'Kake Udon': "Thick wheat noodles in a simple hot broth, the most basic Japanese udon. Kagawa prefecture is so fond of udon it calls itself “Udon prefecture”.",
    'Zaru Soba': "Cold buckwheat noodles from Japan, served on a bamboo tray and dipped in sauce. Japanese people eat soba on New Year's Eve for a long life.",
    'Okonomiyaki': "A savoury cabbage pancake from Osaka and Hiroshima in Japan. The name means “grilled as you like it”; Hiroshima stacks the layers, Osaka mixes them.",
    'Takoyaki': "Batter balls with octopus inside from Osaka, Japan, first sold in 1935. They are turned with picks in special pans with round holes.",
    'Natto Gohan': "Fermented soybeans over rice, a Japanese breakfast. Its strong smell and stringy texture divide even Japan; it is most popular around Tokyo and the north-east.",
    'Daifuku Mochi': "Soft rice cakes filled with sweet red bean paste, from Japan. The rice is pounded in a big mortar with wooden mallets, a New Year custom called mochitsuki.",
    'Matcha Latte': "Matcha is finely ground green tea from Japan, whisked in tea ceremonies since the 1100s, when Zen monks brought powdered tea from China. With milk it became a modern café drink.",
    'Bibimbap': "A Korean rice bowl of vegetables, beef, egg and chili paste, mixed together before eating. Jeonju in South Korea is the most famous home of bibimbap.",
    'Kimchi Jjigae': "A spicy Korean stew made with well-fermented kimchi, pork and tofu. Older, sour kimchi is best for this home-cooked comfort dish.",
    'Kimchi Fried Rice': "A quick Korean dish of fried rice with chopped kimchi, often topped with a fried egg. It is a favourite home lunch in South Korea.",
    'Korean Fried Chicken': "Twice-fried crispy chicken glazed with sweet chili, from South Korea. It boomed after the 1970s and is eaten with beer, a pairing called “chimaek”.",
    'Tteokbokki': "Chewy rice cakes in a sweet and spicy chili sauce, Korea's favourite street food. The red spicy version became popular in Seoul in the 1950s; the older royal court version used soy sauce.",
    'Japchae': "Glass noodles made from sweet potato starch, stir-fried with vegetables, from Korea. It was first made for a royal banquet in the 1600s, and noodles were added later.",
    'Haemul Pajeon': "A Korean pancake of spring onions and seafood. Koreans like to eat it on rainy days, when the sizzle of the pan is said to sound like rain.",
      // South & Southeast Asian
    'Butter Chicken': "Murgh makhani from Delhi, India: tandoori chicken in a buttery tomato sauce. It was created at the Moti Mahal restaurant in the 1940s or 50s, though the families of its two founders have taken their quarrel over who invented it to court.",
    'Chicken Tikka Masala': "Grilled chicken pieces in a creamy, spiced tomato sauce. It grew from Indian and Pakistani cooking in British curry houses and is now one of Britain's most popular dishes.",
    'Tandoori Chicken': "Chicken marinated in yogurt and spices and roasted in a clay oven, from Punjab in northern India and Pakistan. Kundan Lal Gujral made it famous at Moti Mahal in Delhi after 1947.",
    'Paneer Tikka': "Cubes of paneer cheese and vegetables marinated in spiced yogurt and grilled in a tandoor, from Punjab in northern India. Paneer is a fresh cheese that does not melt, made by curdling milk with lemon juice.",
    'Palak Paneer': "Paneer in a smooth, spiced spinach sauce, from Punjab in northern India. It is one of the best-known dishes of India's large vegetarian kitchen.",
    'Saag Aloo': "Potatoes cooked with spinach or other greens, from Punjab and northern India. “Saag” means leafy greens, “aloo” potato; potatoes came to India with Portuguese traders in the 1600s.",
    'Aloo Gobi': "A dry curry of potatoes and cauliflower with turmeric and cumin, from Punjab in northern India and Pakistan. It is everyday home food across the north.",
    'Dal Tadka': "Lentils finished with a tadka: spices sizzled in hot ghee and poured on top. Dal is eaten every day across India, Pakistan, Nepal and Bangladesh, and this version is a classic of North Indian roadside dhabas.",
    'Chana Masala': "Chickpeas in a spicy tomato and onion sauce, from Punjab in northern India and Pakistan. India grows most of the world's chickpeas.",
    'Rajma Chawal': "Red kidney beans in a thick spiced gravy with rice, the Sunday lunch of Punjab and Jammu in northern India. Kidney beans came from the Americas and are now grown in the Himalayan foothills.",
    'Chole Bhature': "Spicy chickpeas with puffy fried bread, a Punjabi breakfast that became Delhi's favourite street food after Punjabi refugees brought it in 1947. The bhature dough is leavened with yogurt and puffs up like a balloon in the hot oil.",
    'Lamb Rogan Josh': "A rich red lamb curry from Kashmir, brought there by Persian-influenced Mughal cooks. The red colour comes from mild Kashmiri chilies and, traditionally, the root of a plant called ratan jot.",
    'Pork Vindaloo': "A sharp, fiery curry from Goa on India's west coast, a Portuguese colony for 450 years. Goan cooks used local chilies and spices in a Portuguese way of preserving meat in vinegar and garlic.",
    'Goan Fish Curry': "Fish in a sour, spicy coconut sauce with tamarind, the everyday meal of Goa in western India, with rice. Mackerel and kingfish come from its Arabian Sea coast.",
    'Machher Jhol': "A light fish stew from Bengal, today West Bengal in India and Bangladesh, where people say “fish and rice make a Bengali”. Mustard seeds and oil give Bengali food its sharpness.",
    'Masala Dosa': "A large, crisp fermented rice and lentil crêpe filled with spiced potato, from Karnataka in South India. Udupi restaurants spread it all over India.",
    'Idli with Sambar': "Steamed rice and lentil cakes with sambar, a tangy lentil and vegetable stew, the breakfast of Tamil Nadu and South India. Idli may have come from Indonesia, where fermentation for steamed foods is old.",
    'Appam with Vegetable Stew': "Bowl-shaped rice pancakes with a mild coconut milk stew, from Kerala in South India. Kerala's Syrian Christians eat it for Easter breakfast.",
    'Pav Bhaji': "A spicy, buttery vegetable mash with soft bread rolls from Mumbai in India. “Pav” comes from the Portuguese word for bread, pão.",
    'Samosas': "Fried triangular pastries filled with spiced potato and peas, eaten across India and Pakistan. They came from Central Asia and Persia as sambosa, filled with meat; Indian cooks later added potato.",
    'Onion Pakora': "Onion slices in spiced chickpea flour batter, deep-fried; a street snack from northern India and Pakistan. They are a monsoon favourite, eaten with tea when it rains.",
    'Garlic Naan': "Soft flatbread slapped against the hot clay wall of a tandoor, then brushed with garlic and ghee. Naan comes from the Mughal and Persian kitchens of northern India and Pakistan.",
    'Momos': "Steamed dumplings with spicy tomato chutney, from Tibet and Nepal. Newar traders of Kathmandu brought the recipe from Lhasa.",
    'Chickpea Coconut Curry': "A modern vegan curry inspired by the coconut-rich cooking of Kerala and Sri Lanka. Coconut palms line the coasts of southern India and the islands of the Indian Ocean.",
    'Kheer': "Rice slowly simmered in milk with sugar and cardamom, one of India's oldest desserts, made for festivals and temples. In Bengal it is called payesh.",
    'Gulab Jamun': "Soft fried milk dough balls soaked in rose-scented syrup, a festival sweet from India, Pakistan and Bangladesh. The name comes from Persian “gulab”, rose water, and “jamun”, a fruit of the same size.",
    'Jalebi': "Crispy spirals of fermented batter fried and soaked in saffron syrup, sold hot in Indian sweet shops. It came from the Persian and Arab zulbiya, brought to India in the Middle Ages.",
    'Mango Lassi': "A yogurt drink blended with mango, from Punjab in northern India. India grows nearly half of the world's mangoes.",
    'Masala Chai': "Black tea boiled with milk, sugar and spices, sold by chai wallahs on Indian streets. Tea plantations in Assam were set up by the British, but India added its own spices.",
    'Pad Thai': "Stir-fried rice noodles with shrimp, tofu, egg and peanuts, from Thailand. The government promoted it as a national dish in the 1930s and 40s, though the noodles came from Chinese cooking.",
    'Thai Green Curry': "A curry from central Thailand made with a paste of fresh green chilies, lemongrass and galangal. It is one of the newest classic Thai curries, appearing in the early 1900s.",
    'Thai Red Curry with Shrimp': "A curry from central Thailand made with a paste of dried red chilies, with coconut milk and Thai basil. Fresh shrimp come from the Gulf of Thailand.",
    'Massaman Curry': "A mild Thai curry with potato and peanuts, linked to Persian and Muslim traders at the old royal court of Ayutthaya. They brought dried spices such as cardamom and cinnamon; the name may come from “Musulman”, Muslim.",
    'Tom Yum Goong': "A hot and sour shrimp soup with lemongrass and galangal from central Thailand. In 2024 UNESCO added tom yum kung to its list of cultural heritage.",
    'Tom Kha Gai': "A creamy chicken soup with coconut milk and galangal from Thailand. Galangal is a ginger-like root with a peppery, citrus taste.",
    'Som Tam': "A salad of shredded green papaya pounded in a clay mortar, from Isan in north-east Thailand and from Laos. It is fiery, sour, salty and sweet all at once.",
    'Cashew Chicken': "Chicken stir-fried with cashews and peppers, in Thailand often with roasted dried chilies. Cashew trees came from Brazil with Portuguese traders.",
    'Mango Sticky Rice': "Glutinous rice soaked in sweet coconut milk with ripe mango, a dessert from Thailand and Laos. It is eaten in mango season from April to June, and in Laos and northern Thailand sticky rice is the everyday staple.",
    'Phở Bò': "Beef noodle soup from Hanoi in northern Vietnam, born around 1900. The clear broth simmers for hours with star anise, cinnamon and charred ginger; after 1954 refugees took it south to Saigon.",
    'Bánh Mì': "A Vietnamese sandwich from Saigon, today Ho Chi Minh City, made on an airy baguette with pork, pickled vegetables and herbs. Vietnamese bakers make the baguette lighter than the French one, often with some rice flour.",
    'Gỏi Cuốn': "Fresh rice paper rolls filled with shrimp, noodles and herbs, from southern Vietnam. They are not fried, so they are also called summer rolls.",
    'Chicken Satay': "Grilled skewers of spiced chicken with peanut sauce from Java in Indonesia, also loved in Malaysia, Singapore and Thailand. Satay may come from Indian kebabs brought by Muslim traders.",
    'Nasi Goreng': "Indonesia's fried rice, sweet with kecap manis soy sauce and savoury with shrimp paste. It was the way to use up the night's leftover rice for breakfast.",
    'Tempeh Buddha Bowl': "A modern health-food bowl built around tempeh, a firm cake of fermented soybeans from Java in Indonesia. Tempeh has been made there for centuries, wrapped in banana leaves.",
    'Hainanese Chicken Rice': "Poached chicken with rice cooked in chicken stock, brought by migrants from Hainan island in China and perfected in Singapore and Malaysia. Singapore counts it among its national dishes.",
    'Chicken Adobo': "Chicken braised in vinegar, soy sauce, garlic and bay leaves, the national dish of the Philippines. Spanish colonists named it adobo, but cooking in vinegar was older than their arrival.",
    'Sinigang': "A sour soup from the Philippines with pork, vegetables and tamarind. Filipinos love sour flavours, which also help food keep in the tropical heat.",
    'Lumpia': "Crispy Filipino spring rolls filled with pork and vegetables, brought by Chinese traders from Fujian. They are a must at Filipino birthday parties.",
    'Taro Coconut Dessert': "A warm dessert of taro and sticky rice in sweet coconut milk, made in Thailand, Vietnam and the Philippines. Taro is one of the oldest crops in Southeast Asia and the Pacific.",
    // The Americas
    'Cheeseburger': "Minced beef patties in a bun came to the USA with German immigrants from Hamburg. The first cheeseburgers are claimed by Pasadena, California, in the 1920s, and several others.",
    'Hot Dog': "A sausage in a bun, the American ballpark snack. The sausages came with German immigrants; frankfurters are from Frankfurt and wieners from Vienna.",
    'Mac and Cheese': "Pasta baked in cheese sauce. The recipe was known in England in the 1700s, but it became an American classic; Thomas Jefferson served macaroni at the White House.",
    'BBQ Pulled Pork': "Pork shoulder smoked for hours until it pulls apart, from the barbecue tradition of North Carolina and the American South. The word barbecue comes from the Taíno people of the Caribbean.",
    'Buffalo Wings': "Fried chicken wings in hot sauce, invented in 1964 at the Anchor Bar in Buffalo, New York, though others claim them too. They come with blue cheese dip and celery.",
    'Southern Fried Chicken': "Buttermilk-marinated fried chicken from the American South. It mixes Scottish frying traditions with the seasoning and skill of enslaved West African cooks.",
    'Cornbread': "Bread made from cornmeal, from the American South. Native Americans taught European settlers to grind and cook maize.",
    'Gumbo': "A thick stew of Louisiana in the USA, with sausage, seafood and okra on a dark roux. It mixes West African, French, Spanish and Choctaw cooking; the name comes from a West African word for okra.",
    'Jambalaya': "A spicy rice dish of Louisiana with chicken, sausage and shrimp, cousin of Spanish paella. Creole jambalaya has tomatoes, Cajun jambalaya does not.",
    "Hoppin' John": "Black-eyed peas and rice from the Lowcountry of South Carolina, rooted in West African cooking. Eating it on New Year's Day is meant to bring luck.",
    'Chili con Carne': "A spicy stew of beef and chilies from Texas, USA, sold by the “chili queens” on the plazas of San Antonio in the 1800s. Many Mexicans see it as American, not Mexican, food.",
    'New England Clam Chowder': "A creamy soup of clams and potatoes from the north-east coast of the USA. In 1939 Maine even proposed a law against adding tomatoes, which is how Manhattan makes it.",
    'Lobster Roll': "Lobster meat in a toasted bun from Maine and New England, USA. Lobster was once so plentiful there that it was thought of as poor people's food.",
    'Maryland Crab Cakes': "Patties of blue crab meat from Chesapeake Bay in Maryland, USA. The best have as little filling as possible, so the sweet crab shines.",
    'Reuben Sandwich': "Corned beef, sauerkraut and Swiss cheese on rye, from the USA. Both a deli in New York and a hotel in Omaha, Nebraska, claim to have invented it.",
    'Pastrami on Rye': "Smoked spiced beef on rye bread, from Jewish delicatessens of New York's Lower East Side. Romanian Jewish immigrants brought pastrami in the late 1800s.",
    'Club Sandwich': "A triple-decker sandwich of turkey, bacon, lettuce and tomato from the USA, first described around 1890. The Saratoga Club House in New York is said to have invented it.",
    'BLT Sandwich': "Bacon, lettuce and tomato on toast, an American lunch counter classic that became popular after the Second World War, when fresh lettuce and tomatoes were available all year. The bacon is fried crisp, and the toasted bread keeps the juicy tomato from making it soggy.",
    'Eggs Benedict': "Poached eggs and ham on English muffins with hollandaise, from New York. Delmonico's restaurant and the Waldorf Hotel both claim it from the late 1800s.",
    'Smoked Salmon Bagel': "Bagels came from Jewish bakers of Kraków in Poland; with smoked salmon and cream cheese they became a New York brunch. In New York the salmon is called lox.",
    'Peanut Butter & Jelly': "The classic American school lunch of peanut butter and jam on white bread. Peanut butter and jelly were part of US soldiers' rations in the Second World War.",
    'Deviled Eggs': "Halved eggs with a spicy, creamy filling, a picnic and party classic in the USA. Stuffed eggs go back to ancient Rome; “deviled” meant spicy in the 1700s.",
    'Caesar Salad': "Romaine lettuce with croutons, parmesan and a dressing of egg, garlic and anchovy. Created by Caesar Cardini at his restaurant in Tijuana, Mexico, where Americans came during Prohibition.",
    'Cobb Salad': "A chopped salad of chicken, bacon, egg, avocado and blue cheese, from the Brown Derby restaurant in Hollywood, USA, in the 1930s. Owner Robert Cobb made it from leftovers.",
    'Kale & Quinoa Salad': "A modern health-food salad from the USA. Quinoa has been grown in the Andes of Bolivia and Peru for 7,000 years; the Incas called it the “mother of all grains”.",
    'Butternut Squash Soup': "A creamy autumn soup from North America. Squash were first grown in the Americas over 8,000 years ago, one of the “three sisters” with corn and beans.",
    'Roast Turkey with Cranberry': "The Thanksgiving dinner of the USA and Canada. Turkeys and cranberries are both native to North America.",
    'Poutine': "Fries with cheese curds and gravy from rural Quebec, Canada, in the 1950s. Several towns claim it; the word may be Quebec slang for “mess”.",
    'Butter Tarts': "Small tarts with a gooey butter, sugar and maple filling, from Ontario, Canada. Some trace them to the “King's daughters”, young French women sent to New France in the 1600s.",
    'Poke Bowl': "Cubed raw fish seasoned with soy and sesame, from Hawaii. It became a bowl with rice and toppings in the 2010s and spread across the world.",
    'Avocado Toast': "Mashed avocado on toast, a modern café brunch from Australia and California. Avocados come from Mexico, where they have been grown for thousands of years.",
    'Corn on the Cob': "Corn was bred from a wild grass called teosinte in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago. Boiled sweet corn with butter is an American summer favourite.",
    'Pancakes': "Thick, fluffy American pancakes with maple syrup, a breakfast classic of the USA and Canada. Settlers learned from Indigenous peoples how to tap maple trees.",
    'Maple Pecan Pancakes': "Buttermilk pancakes with pecans and maple syrup, from Canada and the USA. Canada makes most of the world's maple syrup, mostly in Quebec, by boiling down sap in spring.",
    'French Toast': "Bread soaked in egg and milk and fried, called “pain perdu” (lost bread) in France. The idea goes back to ancient Rome; the name French toast is American.",
    'Overnight Oats': "Rolled oats soaked overnight in milk, a modern American take on the Swiss Bircher muesli. No cooking needed: the oats soften by morning.",
    'Smoothie': "Blended fruit with yogurt, an American drink of the 1930s, when electric blenders came on the market. It became a health craze in California in the 1960s and 70s.",
    'New York Cheesecake': "A dense, rich cheesecake made with cream cheese on a biscuit base, from New York. Cream cheese was first made in New York state in 1872; cheesecakes themselves date back to ancient Greece.",
    'Pecan Pie': "A sweet pie of pecan nuts and syrup, from the American South. Pecans are native to the Mississippi valley; their name comes from an Algonquian word for a nut cracked with a stone.",
    'Pumpkin Pie': "A spiced pumpkin custard pie, eaten at Thanksgiving in the USA and Canada. Pumpkins are native to North America.",
    'Key Lime Pie': "A tart lime and condensed milk pie from the Florida Keys, USA. Condensed milk was used because there was no fresh milk on the islands before refrigeration.",
    'Brownies': "Fudgy chocolate squares from the USA, first created at the Palmer House Hotel in Chicago for the World's Fair of 1893. Bertha Palmer asked for a cake small enough to fit in a lady's lunch box.",
    'Chocolate Chip Cookies': "America's favourite cookie, made with chopped chocolate that keeps its shape in the oven. Chocolate came from Mexico and Central America.",
    'White Chocolate Macadamia Cookies': "An American cookie of the 1980s with white chocolate and macadamia nuts. Macadamias come from the rainforests of Australia and are grown in Hawaii.",
    'Banana Bread': "A moist loaf of overripe bananas, popular in the USA since the 1930s, when baking soda and baking powder became common. It was a good way to use up old bananas during the Great Depression.",
    'Banana Split': "A banana with ice cream, chocolate sauce, cream and cherries, invented in 1904 in Latrobe, Pennsylvania, USA, by a young pharmacist. Wilmington, Ohio, claims it too.",
    "S'mores": "A campfire treat from the USA: toasted marshmallow and chocolate between graham crackers. The first recipe was published by the Girl Scouts in 1927.",
    'Strawberry Milkshake': "Milk blended with ice cream and strawberries, an American soda fountain classic. Electric milkshake mixers appeared in the USA in the 1910s.",
    'Lemonade': "Lemon juice, sugar and water. Lemonade was sold in medieval Cairo and in 1600s Paris; in the USA it is a summer drink sold at children's lemonade stands.",
    'Iced Coffee': "Coffee poured over ice with sweet condensed milk, popular across the Americas and Asia. Vietnam, Thailand and the USA each have their own famous version.",
    'Oat Milk Latte': "Espresso with oat milk, a modern plant-based café drink. Oat milk was developed in Sweden in the 1990s and became popular in the USA in the late 2010s.",
    'Bloody Mary': "A cocktail of tomato juice and vodka with spices, claimed by Harry's New York Bar in Paris in the 1920s and by the St. Regis Hotel in New York. Tomatoes come from the Andes.",
    'Tacos al Pastor': "Pork marinated in chilies and achiote, roasted on a vertical spit and served in corn tortillas with pineapple. A classic of Mexico City, born in the 1950s and 60s from Lebanese shawarma.",
    'Fish Tacos': "Fried or grilled fish in tortillas with cabbage and cream, from the Pacific coast of Baja California, Mexico. Ensenada is famous for its fish taco stands.",
    'Guacamole': "Mashed avocado with lime, chili and onion, first made by the Aztecs in Mexico. The name comes from the Nahuatl word “ahuacamolli”, avocado sauce.",
    'Nachos': "Fried tortilla chips with melted cheese and jalapeños, invented in 1943 in Piedras Negras, Mexico, by Ignacio “Nacho” Anaya. He made them for American women from across the border who arrived after the kitchen had closed.",
    'Quesadillas': "Tortillas folded around melted cheese, from central Mexico. The name comes from “queso”, cheese, though in Mexico City you have to ask for cheese in them.",
    'Chicken Enchiladas': "Corn tortillas rolled around chicken and covered in chili sauce, from Mexico. The Aztecs already ate corn tortillas rolled around fish.",
    'Beef Burrito': "A big flour tortilla wrapped around beans, rice and meat, from northern Mexico. The modern stuffed burrito was perfected in the Mission District of San Francisco in the 1960s.",
    'Chilaquiles': "Fried tortilla pieces simmered in salsa with egg and cheese, a Mexican breakfast. It was a way to use up stale tortillas; the name comes from Nahuatl for “chilies and greens”.",
    'Tamales': "Corn dough with filling, steamed in corn husks, from Mesoamerica, where they have been made for thousands of years. In Mexico they are eaten at Christmas and on Candlemas.",
    'Pozole Rojo': "A Mexican soup of large hominy corn kernels and pork in red chili broth, from Jalisco and Guerrero. It was once a ceremonial dish of the Aztecs.",
    'Chiles Rellenos': "Poblano peppers stuffed with cheese, dipped in egg batter and fried, from Puebla in Mexico. Poblano chilies are named after Puebla.",
    'Elote': "Grilled corn on a stick coated with mayonnaise, cheese, chili and lime, a Mexican street snack. Elote comes from the Nahuatl word “elotl”, tender corn.",
    'Tres Leches Cake': "A sponge cake soaked in three kinds of milk, eaten across Mexico and Central America, where Nicaragua claims it as its own. Its popularity may have come from recipes printed on condensed milk cans.",
    'Hot Chocolate': "The Maya and Aztecs of Mexico drank chocolate frothy and bitter, with chili. Spanish traders brought it to Europe in the 1500s, where sugar and milk were added.",
    'Ceviche': "Raw fish cured in lime juice with chili and onion, Peru's national dish, eaten along the Pacific coast of Latin America. Peru celebrates National Ceviche Day on 28 June.",
    'Causa Limeña': "Layers of yellow potato mash seasoned with lime and chili, filled with tuna and avocado, from Lima in Peru. One story says it fed soldiers during the War of the Pacific.",
    'Lomo Saltado': "A stir-fry of beef, tomato and onion with soy sauce, served with both fries and rice, from Peru. It shows how Chinese immigrants, who came to Peru from the 1850s, mixed their wok cooking with local ingredients.",
    'Arepas Reina Pepiada': "Cornmeal pockets from Venezuela filled with chicken and avocado salad. The filling was named “curvy queen” in honour of Susana Duijm, Venezuela's first Miss World in 1955.",
    'Empanadas': "Folded pastries filled with meat, eggs and olives, eaten across Latin America, especially in Argentina and Chile. They came from Galicia in Spain, and their name means “wrapped in bread”.",
    'Asado with Chimichurri': "Argentina's barbecue: big cuts of beef slowly grilled over embers, with chimichurri, a sauce of parsley, garlic and vinegar. The gauchos of the pampas made it the national tradition; Uruguay shares it.",
    'Churrasco': "Brazilian barbecue from the gaucho cowboys of Rio Grande do Sul in the south. Large skewers of beef seasoned only with coarse salt are grilled over fire, served with toasted cassava flour.",
    'Alfajores': "Soft biscuits sandwiched with dulce de leche, the favourite sweet of Argentina, Uruguay and Peru. The name and the idea come from Spain, where alfajores were an Arab-influenced sweet.",
    'Pão de Queijo': "Small chewy cheese breads from Minas Gerais in Brazil. They are made with cassava starch instead of wheat, which gives them their stretchy bite.",
    'Brigadeiros': "Brazilian truffles of condensed milk, cocoa and butter, named after Brigadier Eduardo Gomes, who ran for president in 1945. They are a must at Brazilian birthday parties.",
    'Moqueca': "A fish stew cooked in a clay pot with coconut milk and dendê palm oil, from Bahia in Brazil. Espírito Santo has its own version without palm oil and argues it is the original.",
    'Jerk Chicken': "Chicken marinated in fiery Scotch bonnet chili and allspice, from Jamaica. The Maroons, people who escaped slavery into the mountains, developed jerk cooking with Taíno techniques.",
    'Rice and Peas': "Rice cooked with kidney beans in coconut milk, Jamaica's Sunday side dish. It reached the Caribbean with West African cooks and is eaten across the islands.",
    'Ackee and Saltfish': "Jamaica's national dish: ackee fruit cooked with salt cod. Ackee came to Jamaica from West Africa in the 1700s; it must be eaten only when fully ripe, as the unripe fruit is poisonous.",
    'Doubles': "Two fried flatbreads with curried chickpeas from Trinidad and Tobago, named for the double serving. Created in the 1930s by a family of Indian descent in Princes Town.",
    'Fried Plantains': "Ripe plantains fried until sweet and caramelised, eaten across the Caribbean, Latin America and West Africa. Plantains first came from South-East Asia by way of Africa; in Cuba and Puerto Rico they are called maduros.",
    'Mojito': "Rum, lime, sugar and mint, from Havana in Cuba. Ernest Hemingway helped make it famous; an older drink called “El Draque” was named after the English privateer Francis Drake.",
    'Piña Colada': "Rum, pineapple and coconut cream from Puerto Rico, the island's national drink since 1978. Two bars in San Juan both claim to have invented it in the 1950s and 60s.",
      // Haute Cuisine
    'Steak au Poivre': "A Parisian bistro classic from France: steak pressed into crushed peppercorns, seared, then sauced with cream and flamed cognac. Several Paris restaurants claimed it in the early 1900s.",
    'Truffle Omelette': "A soft French omelette perfumed with shaved black truffle from Périgord in south-west France. Truffles grow underground on tree roots and are sniffed out by trained dogs or pigs.",
    'Porcini Risotto': "Creamy rice from northern Italy, stirred slowly with stock and wild porcini mushrooms gathered in autumn forests. Risotto rice grows in the flooded paddies of Piedmont and Lombardy.",
    'Risotto alla Milanese': "Milan's golden risotto from Lombardy, Italy, coloured with saffron: each thread is the hand-picked stigma of a crocus flower. Legend says it began with a glass painter's apprentice who coloured his rice with saffron at a wedding in 1574.",
    'Chicken with Morel Cream': "A French dish from the Jura in eastern France: chicken in a cream sauce with morels and yellow wine. Morels are rare spring mushrooms that are very hard to farm and are mostly gathered in the wild.",
    'Vitello Tonnato': "A cold dish from Piedmont in northern Italy: thinly sliced poached veal under a smooth tuna and caper sauce. It is a summer and Christmas favourite in Italy and Argentina, where Italian immigrants took it.",
    'Steak Béarnaise': "Steak with béarnaise, a warm emulsion of egg yolks and butter flavoured with tarragon, shallot and vinegar. The sauce was created near Paris in the 1830s and named after Béarn, the home region of King Henri IV.",
    'Halibut with Beurre Blanc': "Delicate white fish with beurre blanc, a sauce from Nantes on the Loire in western France, made by whisking cold butter into a wine reduction. Legend says a cook invented it when she forgot the eggs for a béarnaise.",
    'Steak Tartare': "Finely chopped raw beef seasoned with capers, shallot and a raw egg yolk, a French brasserie classic of the 1900s. Its name is linked to the Tatars of the Central Asian steppes, though the story that they tenderised meat under their saddles is a myth.",
    'Bouillabaisse': "The fishermen's stew of Marseille in southern France, made from several kinds of fish in a saffron and fennel broth, served with rouille. Marseille's chefs even wrote a charter in 1980 to define the real recipe.",
    'Beef Wellington': "Beef fillet wrapped in mushroom duxelles and ham, then baked in puff pastry. It is called British and linked to the Duke of Wellington, but no proof of that has been found, and France has the similar filet de boeuf en croûte.",
    'Sole Meunière': "Sole dusted in flour and fried in browned butter with lemon, from France: “meunière” means “the miller's wife's way”. Julia Child said it was her first meal in France, in Rouen in 1948.",
    "Duck à l'Orange": "Roast duck with a bittersweet orange sauce, a classic of French cuisine. The pairing came from Italian cooks of the Renaissance, said to have travelled with Catherine de' Medici to France.",
    'Baked Brie en Croûte': "A whole wheel of soft Brie wrapped in pastry and baked until it flows when cut. Brie comes from the Île-de-France region east of Paris and was crowned the “king of cheeses” at the Congress of Vienna in 1815.",
    'Beef Carpaccio': "Paper-thin raw beef with olive oil and parmesan, invented at Harry's Bar in Venice, Italy, in 1950. It was named after the painter Vittore Carpaccio, famous for his reds.",
    'Boeuf Bourguignon': "The beef stew of Burgundy in eastern France, braised for hours in the region's red wine with mushrooms, bacon and small onions. It began as a farmers' dish for tough old cattle.",
    'Oysters Mignonette': "Raw oysters on ice with mignonette, a sharp sauce of shallots, vinegar and cracked pepper, a French brasserie classic. Oysters from Brittany and Arcachon take about three years to grow.",
    'Cheese Soufflé': "A French dish of beaten egg whites and cheese sauce that rises high in the oven and must be served at once. The chef Marie-Antoine Carême made soufflés famous in early 1800s Paris; the name means “puffed up”.",
    'Trout Amandine': "Trout fried in butter and finished with toasted flaked almonds, a classic of French cooking. French settlers took it to New Orleans, where it became a Creole favourite.",
    'Osso Buco': "Braised veal shanks from Milan in Lombardy, Italy, cut across the bone so the marrow melts into the sauce. It is topped with gremolata and served with saffron risotto.",
    'Quail Egg Canapés': "Tiny bites on toast crowned with quail eggs, a staple of French-style cocktail receptions. Canapé is French for sofa: the topping sits on the bread as if on a couch.",
    'Blini with Salmon Roe': "Small yeasted buckwheat pancakes from Russia with sour cream and glistening salmon roe. Russians eat stacks of blini at Maslenitsa, the week before Lent, as round golden symbols of the sun.",
    'Duck Confit': "Duck legs salted, then cooked slowly in their own fat: an old way of keeping meat through the winter in Gascony, south-west France. Confit comes from the French word for preserved.",
    // Pâtisserie & Chocolaterie
    'Black Forest Cake': "Chocolate sponge layered with cherries, cream and kirsch from Germany, named after the cherry brandy of the Black Forest in Baden-Württemberg. The confectioner Josef Keller claimed to have created it in Bad Godesberg in 1915.",
    'Lamingtons': "Australian squares of sponge cake dipped in chocolate and rolled in coconut, named after Lord Lamington, governor of Queensland around 1900. New Zealand has claimed them too, but the Queensland story is the best known.",
    'Sachertorte': "Vienna's famous chocolate cake with apricot jam under a glossy chocolate glaze, created by the 16-year-old apprentice Franz Sacher in 1832. The Hotel Sacher and the Demel bakery fought in court for years over the right to sell the “original”.",
    'Tiramisu': "Italian layers of coffee-soaked biscuits and mascarpone cream dusted with cocoa. The name means “pick me up”; Treviso in Veneto and Friuli both claim it, from the 1950s to the 1970s.",
    'Panforte': "A dense cake of honey, nuts, candied fruit and spices from Siena in Tuscany, Italy, known since the 1200s. Crusaders and medieval travellers carried it for months.",
    'Crêpes Suzette': "Thin crêpes in a buttery orange and caramel sauce, flamed with orange liqueur at the table. French legend says a young waiter set the sauce alight by accident in Monte Carlo in 1895 while serving the future King Edward VII.",
    'Pain au Chocolat': "Laminated, buttery croissant dough rolled around bars of dark chocolate, from France. In south-west France it is called a chocolatine, and the name is a heated debate.",
    'Chocolate Mousse': "Melted dark chocolate lightened with whipped eggs into an airy French dessert. The painter Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec, a keen cook, was one of its early fans and called it “mayonnaise de chocolat”.",
    'Cannoli': "Fried pastry tubes filled with sweet ricotta cream from Sicily, Italy, once made for Carnival. They are linked to Palermo and the Arab rule of Sicily, when sugar cane was brought to the island.",
    'Christmas Stollen': "A rich fruit bread from Dresden in Saxony, Germany, with rum-soaked raisins, candied peel and marzipan, dusted white like snow. Stollen is recorded in Dresden as early as 1474, and the city's Christmas market, the Striezelmarkt, is named after it.",
    'Éclairs': "Long choux pastry fingers filled with pastry cream and glazed, from 1800s France, probably created by Marie-Antoine Carême. They are so good they are gone “in a flash”: éclair means lightning.",
    'Profiteroles': "Little choux puffs filled with cream or ice cream and covered in warm chocolate sauce, from France. Choux pastry is often credited to a cook of Catherine de' Medici in the 1500s.",
    'Macarons': "Delicate almond meringue shells sandwiching ganache. Almond macarons came to France from Italy; the double-decker Parisian macaron was popularised by the Ladurée tea room in Paris in the 1900s.",
    'Crème Brûlée': "A rich vanilla custard under a thin sheet of sugar burnt to glassy caramel just before serving. France, England (Cambridge's “burnt cream”) and Catalonia (crema catalana) all claim it.",
    'Baklava': "Layers of paper-thin filo pastry with chopped nuts, soaked in syrup, perfected in the kitchens of the Ottoman palace in Istanbul. Turkey, Greece and the Middle East all make it; Gaziantep's pistachio baklava is protected by an EU origin label.",
    'Mille-feuille': "“A thousand leaves” from France: three layers of crisp puff pastry with vanilla cream and a feathered icing top. Its modern form appeared in 1800s Paris; Italy calls it millefoglie.",
    'Engadiner Nusstorte': "A shortcrust pie filled with caramelised walnuts and cream from the Engadin valley in Graubünden, Switzerland. It was made famous in the 1900s by Engadin pastry makers who had worked abroad.",
    'Croissants': "Crescent rolls of yeasted dough folded again and again with butter to make dozens of flaky layers. They come from the Austrian kipferl, brought to Paris in 1839 by the Viennese bakery of August Zang; the French made them flaky.",
    'Pastel de Nata': "Portuguese custard tarts in crisp pastry, created by monks of the Jerónimos Monastery in Belém, Lisbon. The monks used the egg yolks left over from starching their habits with egg whites.",
    'Paris-Brest': "A ring of choux pastry filled with praline cream, from France. It was created in 1910 and shaped like a bicycle wheel to celebrate the Paris to Brest cycle race.",
    'Mont Blanc': "Sweet chestnut purée piped in strands over meringue and cream to look like the snowy peak of Mont Blanc on the French-Italian border. Paris's Angelina tea room made it famous in the 1900s; Italy calls it Monte Bianco.",
    'Tarte au Citron': "A crisp sweet pastry case filled with a tangy, silky lemon curd, from France. Menton on the French Riviera is so proud of its lemons that it holds a lemon festival every February.",
    'Peach Melba': "Poached peaches with vanilla ice cream and raspberry sauce, created around 1892 by the French chef Auguste Escoffier at the Savoy Hotel in London. It honours the Australian opera singer Nellie Melba.",
    'Nougat Montélimar': "Chewy white nougat from Montélimar in Provence, France, made of honey, whipped egg whites, almonds and pistachios. Almond trees were planted around the town in the 1600s.",
    // Grand Banquets
    'Bulgogi': "Korean “fire meat”: thin slices of beef marinated in soy, sugar, garlic and pear, grilled over flame. It goes back to the Goguryeo kingdom over 1,500 years ago and was a royal favourite.",
    'Persian Jeweled Rice': "A wedding dish from Iran of saffron rice studded with barberries, orange peel, pistachios and almonds like jewels. Its sweet and sour toppings are wishes for a sweet married life.",
    'Chicken Biryani': "Layered rice and spiced chicken cooked sealed in one pot, a feast dish of the Mughal courts of India. Hyderabad, Lucknow and Kolkata each have their own famous style.",
    'Nigiri Sushi': "Hand-pressed fingers of vinegared rice topped with slices of raw fish, invented in Edo, today's Tokyo, in the 1820s as fast food. Sushi chefs train for years.",
    'Malai Kofta': "Fried dumplings of paneer and potato in a rich cream sauce, a vegetarian dish from the Mughal kitchen of northern India. “Malai” means cream.",
    'Peking Duck': "Imperial Beijing's roast duck with lacquered crisp skin, sliced at the table and wrapped in thin pancakes. The oldest Beijing duck restaurant, Bianyifang, opened in 1416.",
    'Tempura': "Seafood and vegetables in an ice-cold, feather-light batter, fried in seconds, from Japan. Portuguese missionaries brought the idea to Nagasaki in the 1500s.",
    'Laksa': "A spicy coconut noodle soup from Malaysia and Singapore, built on a paste of chillies, lemongrass and shrimp. It comes from the Peranakan people, descendants of Chinese traders who married local Malays.",
    'Doro Wat': "Ethiopia's festive chicken stew, rich with berbere spice and slow-cooked onions, served with eggs on injera bread. It breaks the long Lenten fast at Easter in Ethiopia and Eritrea.",
    'Nihari': "A beef stew slow-cooked overnight and eaten at dawn, from Old Delhi in India and today a pride of Pakistan. Its name comes from the Arabic word for morning.",
    'Lamb Tagine with Apricots': "Lamb slowly simmered with apricots and spices from Morocco. It is cooked in a tagine, a clay pot whose cone-shaped lid sends the steam back down into the stew.",
    'Mole Poblano': "Mexico's festive sauce from Puebla: dozens of ingredients including several chillies, spices and chocolate. Legend says nuns of the Santa Rosa convent invented it in the 1600s for a visiting archbishop.",
    'Unadon': "Grilled freshwater eel glazed with sweet soy sauce over rice, from Japan, popular since Edo times. Japanese people eat eel on the hottest days of summer for strength.",
    'Sashimi Platter': "Slices of the freshest raw fish from Japan, cut with a single stroke of a long knife and arranged like a painting. Unlike sushi, sashimi is served without rice.",
    'Uni Don': "A bowl of rice topped with sea urchin roe, a briny and creamy delicacy from Hokkaido in northern Japan. Divers pick the sea urchins off the rocks by hand.",
    'Beef Rendang': "A beef curry from the Minangkabau people of West Sumatra in Indonesia, cooked for hours in coconut milk and spices until almost dry. Malaysia also cooks it for weddings and festivals.",
    'Mooncakes': "Chinese pastries with a lotus seed filling and often a salted egg yolk “moon”, given at the Mid-Autumn Festival. They are eaten wherever Chinese communities live, from Hong Kong to Singapore.",
    'Shabu-shabu': "A Japanese hot pot from Osaka in the 1950s: diners swish paper-thin slices of beef through simmering broth. The name is the swishing sound it makes; it was inspired by Chinese hot pot.",
    'Steamed Fish with Ginger': "A whole fish steamed with ginger and spring onion, a Cantonese banquet dish from southern China. In Chinese “fish” sounds like “abundance”, so it is served at New Year.",
    'Feijoada': "Brazil's national stew of black beans with smoked pork and sausages, served with rice and orange slices. It comes from Portuguese bean stews; the story that enslaved Africans invented it is popular but disputed.",
    'Tonkotsu Ramen': "Ramen in a milky pork bone broth boiled for many hours, from Fukuoka prefecture on the island of Kyushu in southern Japan. It is said to have started from a mistake: a broth left boiling too long.",
    'Fesenjan': "A stew from Iran of chicken or duck in a sweet-sour sauce of ground walnuts and pomegranate molasses. It comes from the Caspian provinces of northern Iran, where pomegranates and walnuts grow.",
    'Xiaolongbao': "Soup dumplings from Nanxiang near Shanghai in China: thin pleated wrappers around pork and jellied broth that melts when steamed. They were first sold there in the 1870s.",
  };
})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window.FS : globalThis.FS);
